WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.401
A shop sends its parcels through a courier.

2
00:00:05.401 --> 00:00:14.447
Most parcels carry no label, and the courier handles them however is quickest: it may bundle them, hold some back, or deliver them out of order.

3
00:00:14.567 --> 00:00:17.568
A few carry a label, like signature required.

4
00:00:17.568 --> 00:00:22.769
Each label costs the courier time, so nobody stamps fragile on a bag of socks.

5
00:00:22.769 --> 00:00:27.237
A sensible shop uses the lightest label that keeps the parcel safe.

6
00:00:27.967 --> 00:00:33.100
Threads send each other parcels too: every read or write of a shared field is one.

7
00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:42.552
The courier is everything in between: the JIT, which may move or skip your reads and writes, and the processor, which may hold writes back for a while.

8
00:00:42.552 --> 00:00:46.120
The label on one read or write is called its access mode.

9
00:00:46.833 --> 00:00:48.509
You know one label already.

10
00:00:48.509 --> 00:00:55.273
A volatile field stamps the heaviest label on every read and write of that field, whether it needs it or not.

11
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:57.336
Usually that's fine.

12
00:00:57.336 --> 00:01:02.880
But picture a mailbox where one thread hands another millions of payments a second.

13
00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:10.895
On Intel and AMD processors, every volatile write waits for its outbox tray, the store buffer from the office, to empty.

14
00:01:10.895 --> 00:01:13.968
Millions of times a second, that wait adds up.

15
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:19.548
Since JDK 9, a VarHandle lets you pick the label for each access instead.

16
00:01:19.548 --> 00:01:23.732
It's a handle to one field, with one pair of methods per label.

17
00:01:23.732 --> 00:01:31.104
There are four labels, and they form a ladder: each keeps every promise of the one below it, and adds one more.

18
00:01:31.233 --> 00:01:34.424
Which raises a question people are shy to ask.

19
00:01:34.424 --> 00:01:37.892
Isn't a lighter label just volatile at a discount?

20
00:01:37.892 --> 00:01:39.765
Same promises, lower price?

21
00:01:40.367 --> 00:01:43.681
The bottom rung is plain: no label, an ordinary field.

22
00:01:43.681 --> 00:01:47.242
It promises nothing about what other threads see, or when.

23
00:01:47.242 --> 00:01:49.636
Plain might as well be called whenever.

24
00:01:49.636 --> 00:01:55.099
The JIT may read it once for a whole loop, like the worker reading stop from its notepad.

25
00:01:55.099 --> 00:01:58.107
A long or a double can even arrive in two halves.

26
00:01:58.233 --> 00:01:59.698
One rung up is opaque.

27
00:01:59.698 --> 00:02:03.494
Its label says: deliver for sure, in order, in one piece.

28
00:02:03.494 --> 00:02:09.154
Every opaque read really happens, so a spinning thread eventually sees the new value.

29
00:02:09.154 --> 00:02:10.952
But it orders nothing else.

30
00:02:10.952 --> 00:02:15.081
A reader can see a flag before the data the flag was guarding.

31
00:02:15.081 --> 00:02:19.009
On ARM, a stress test caught that in 0.68 percent of tries.

32
00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:21.006
Next comes release and acquire.

33
00:02:21.006 --> 00:02:25.294
A release write says: deliver everything I sent before this one, first.

34
00:02:25.294 --> 00:02:32.241
A reader who signs for it with an acquire, and sees the new value, also sees everything written before the release.

35
00:02:32.241 --> 00:02:36.952
That's a happens-before edge, like the cashier's key, built from one variable.

36
00:02:37.067 --> 00:02:38.800
The top rung is volatile.

37
00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:48.643
It adds one more promise: every thread agrees on one order of all volatile reads and writes, like registered mail, logged in one central book.

38
00:02:49.367 --> 00:02:51.565
What does that last promise buy?

39
00:02:51.565 --> 00:02:53.832
Back to the test from the office.

40
00:02:53.832 --> 00:02:56.512
Thread A writes 1 into x, then reads y.

41
00:02:56.512 --> 00:02:59.191
Thread B writes 1 into y, then reads x.

42
00:02:59.191 --> 00:03:02.627
With release and acquire, can both threads read 0?

43
00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:13.071
Here's one run of the demo, 262,144 tries per label.

44
00:03:13.071 --> 00:03:18.531
Plain: 97,635 tries where both read 0.

45
00:03:18.531 --> 00:03:20.686
Opaque: 94,344.

46
00:03:20.686 --> 00:03:24.709
Release and acquire: 92,854.

47
00:03:24.709 --> 00:03:26.433
Volatile: 0.

48
00:03:26.567 --> 00:03:28.189
Well, that's not a discount.

49
00:03:28.189 --> 00:03:29.754
That's a different product.

50
00:03:29.754 --> 00:03:34.332
Release keeps earlier writes before it, and acquire keeps later reads after it.

51
00:03:34.332 --> 00:03:40.880
Neither stops a thread's read from going ahead of its own write, while that write still waits in the outbox tray.

52
00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:42.792
Only the central book forbids it.

53
00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:45.858
And here the parcel picture breaks.

54
00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:48.754
Real labels cost more as they get stronger.

55
00:03:48.754 --> 00:03:57.444
On Intel and AMD, plain, opaque, release and acquire all become ordinary reads and writes; those labels mostly hold back the JIT.

56
00:03:57.444 --> 00:04:02.496
Only the volatile write adds an instruction, the one that empties the tray.

57
00:04:02.496 --> 00:04:06.403
Three labels at one price, and a surcharge for the fourth.

58
00:04:07.133 --> 00:04:09.067
So each label adds one promise.

59
00:04:09.067 --> 00:04:13.995
Pick the lightest label whose promise your code needs, and never a lighter one.

60
00:04:16.067 --> 00:04:19.326
And in everyday code, you almost never pick at all.

61
00:04:19.326 --> 00:04:24.759
Use a ready-made concurrent collection, a volatile field, an atomic class, or a lock.

62
00:04:24.759 --> 00:04:32.684
The lighter labels belong in low-level building blocks, like a fast queue, after a measurement says volatile costs too much.

63
00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:37.689
Even then, a green test on your laptop only proves your laptop is polite.

64
00:04:37.689 --> 00:04:43.516
Intel and AMD never let one write pass another, so many ordering bugs can't show there.

65
00:04:43.516 --> 00:04:48.137
You need a stress tester that runs millions of tries, ideally on ARM.

66
00:04:48.867 --> 00:04:51.822
In code, each label is a method on the handle.

67
00:04:51.822 --> 00:04:57.733
Java on the left, Kotlin on the right: the release write and the acquire read from the test.

68
00:04:58.433 --> 00:05:01.934
So, is release and acquire volatile at a discount?

69
00:05:01.934 --> 00:05:02.144
No.

70
00:05:02.144 --> 00:05:09.284
It's a lighter label with one promise fewer, and that missing promise is exactly the both-zero result.

71
00:05:09.284 --> 00:05:10.684
Weaker, not cheaper.

72
00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:11.580
Quick check.

73
00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:15.414
A writer fills in a report, then sets ready with setOpaque.

74
00:05:15.414 --> 00:05:19.119
Can a reader that sees ready find the report still empty?
